{"meta":{"course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"03","section":"01","chapter_title":"Transparency and Accountability","title":"Module 1: Black Boxes, Interpretability, and the Right to Explanation","description":"Distinguish interpretability from explainability, evaluate post-hoc explanation methods, and meet the legal-and-ethical floor for meaningful explanations under GDPR, EU AI Act, and FCRA.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ai-ethics-03-01-lo-01","statement":"Distinguish interpretability (property of the model) from explainability (property of the explanation surface)"},{"id":"ai-ethics-03-01-lo-02","statement":"Compare feature attribution, counterfactual, and example-based explanation methods and their limits"},{"id":"ai-ethics-03-01-lo-03","statement":"State three properties of a meaningful explanation (faithful, actionable, contextually appropriate) and apply them to a real case"},{"id":"ai-ethics-03-01-lo-04","statement":"Identify the relevant regulatory framework (GDPR Art. 22, EU AI Act, FCRA) for a given deployment context"}],"concept_ids":["interpretability","explainability","post_hoc_explanation","counterfactual_explanation","right_to_explanation","gdpr_article_22"],"prereq_section_ids":["ai-ethics/02/02"],"practice_ids":["explain_interp_vs_explain","explain_counterfactual_value","explain_post_hoc_limit","explain_high_stakes_choice"],"estimated_time_minutes":13,"order":1,"lo_practice_map":{"ai-ethics-03-01-lo-01":["explain_interp_vs_explain","explain_high_stakes_choice"],"ai-ethics-03-01-lo-02":["explain_counterfactual_value"],"ai-ethics-03-01-lo-03":["explain_counterfactual_value","explain_post_hoc_limit"],"ai-ethics-03-01-lo-04":["explain_counterfactual_value"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Black Boxes, Interpretability, and the Right to Explanation\n\n> **\"You do not get to hide behind the algorithm. If the model made the decision, someone still has to be able to explain it.\"**\n\n---\n\n## Opening: When \"The Algorithm Decided\" Stopped Being an Answer\n\nIn November 2019, software developer David Heinemeier Hansson posted that he had been granted a credit limit roughly twenty times higher than his wife on the newly launched Apple Card, despite the couple filing joint tax returns and her having the higher credit score. Steve Wozniak, Apple's co-founder, reported the same pattern with his own spouse. The card's issuer, Goldman Sachs, responded that the underwriting model did not use gender as an input, so it could not be discriminating.\n\nThat defense missed the point, and the New York Department of Financial Services opened an investigation. A model can produce gender-disparate outcomes without ever receiving \"gender\" as a feature, because other inputs act as proxies. More importantly, when applicants and regulators asked *why* a specific limit was assigned, neither the customer service line nor the issuer could give a faithful, decision-specific reason. The system was opaque even to the people defending it. The 2021 DFS report did not find the model intentionally used prohibited characteristics, but it sharply criticized the lack of transparency and the inability to explain individual decisions to customers.\n\nThat is the recurring shape of the modern transparency problem. For most of computing history, the question \"why did the system make this decision?\" had a tractable answer: read the code. A neural network with billions of parameters does not have a \"reason\" in any sense a human can audit. The decision lives in distributed weights, and no individual weight contains the explanation.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** \"We did not use the protected attribute\" and \"we cannot explain the decision\" are increasingly treated by regulators as two failures, not one defense. This module is about closing both gaps.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Distinguish** interpretability (a property of the model) from explainability (a property of the explanation surface).\n2. **Compare** feature-attribution, counterfactual, and example-based explanation methods, and state the limit of each.\n3. **Evaluate** an explanation against three properties (faithful, actionable, contextually appropriate) using a structured decision analysis.\n4. **Identify** the relevant regulatory framework (GDPR Article 22, EU AI Act, FCRA) for a given deployment context.\n\n---\n\n## Interpretability vs. Explainability\n\nThe two terms are often used interchangeably but mean different things in the literature, and the difference drives design choices.\n\n**Interpretability** is a property of the model: the degree to which a human can understand the cause of a decision by inspecting the model itself. Linear models, decision trees, and small rule lists are intrinsically interpretable. Deep neural networks generally are not.\n\n**Explainability** is a property of the *explanation surface* offered around the model: the degree to which a human can understand the model's behavior through generated rationale, feature attributions, counterfactuals, or example-based justification. A black-box model can be explainable without being interpretable.\n\nThis distinction matters for accountability. Interpretable models are auditable by their structure; you check the thing that actually decides. Explainable systems are auditable only by their explanation layer, so the quality of the explanation itself becomes a separate artifact under review, and it can be wrong about the model it claims to describe.\n\n| | Interpretable model | Explainable black box |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What you audit | The model's structure | A generated explanation |\n| Failure mode | Model too simple for the task | Explanation unfaithful to model |\n| Example | Decision tree, logistic regression | Deep net + SHAP report |\n| Who guarantees accuracy | The model is the proof | You must separately validate the explainer |\n\n---\n\n## Glass-Box, Black-Box, and Why It's Not a Binary\n\nA useful taxonomy comes from Rudin (2019, *Nature Machine Intelligence*):\n\n**Inherently interpretable models** (glass-box): linear regression, generalized additive models, small decision trees, RuleFit, explainable boosting machines. A human reading the model can usually reconstruct the decision.\n\n**Black-box models with post-hoc explanations**: deep networks, large ensembles, LLMs. The model is not directly readable; an explanation method (LIME, SHAP, integrated gradients, counterfactuals) is layered on top.\n\nRudin's central argument: **for high-stakes decisions, prefer inherently interpretable models when their accuracy is competitive.** Many published comparisons that justify black-box use overstate the accuracy gap and ignore the cost of unauditable explanations. The choice is not \"accuracy versus interpretability\" by default; it is a tradeoff that must be measured, not assumed.\n\nTo make the tradeoff concrete, model a deployment as a single \"defensibility\" score that combines the accuracy you gain from a black box against the audit cost you take on. Let `acc_gap` be the measured percentage-point accuracy advantage of the black box over the best glass-box model, `stakes` a 1-to-10 severity of a wrong decision, and `audit_cost` the per-decision cost (in effort units) of validating a post-hoc explanation. A net defensibility of choosing the black box can be written as the accuracy benefit minus the stakes-weighted audit burden. Push the accuracy gap toward zero (Rudin's empirical claim for tabular high-stakes tasks) and watch the black box stop paying for itself.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Black-Box vs Glass-Box Defensibility\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"acc_gap\",\"label\":\"Black-box accuracy advantage\",\"min\":0,\"max\":10,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"pp\",\"default\":2},{\"name\":\"stakes\",\"label\":\"Decision severity\",\"min\":1,\"max\":10,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"/10\",\"default\":8},{\"name\":\"audit_cost\",\"label\":\"Per-decision audit cost\",\"min\":0,\"max\":5,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"units\",\"default\":3}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Net defensibility of black box\",\"unit\":\"score\",\"expr\":\"acc_gap - stakes * audit_cost / 5\"},{\"label\":\"Stakes-weighted audit burden\",\"unit\":\"score\",\"expr\":\"stakes * audit_cost / 5\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"acc_gap\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n---\n\n## Post-Hoc Explanation Methods and Their Limits\n\nThree families of post-hoc explanation are widely used.\n\n**Feature attribution** (LIME, SHAP, integrated gradients) tells you which input features most influenced a particular output. Useful for sanity-checking (\"did the model lean on a protected proxy?\"), but the explanation is local and can be unstable: small input changes can flip which features appear important.\n\n**Counterfactual explanations** answer \"what minimal change to the input would have flipped the decision?\" This aligns well with how humans naturally reason about causation. But counterfactuals are not unique; multiple minimal changes can flip the same decision, and which one to surface is itself a design and ethics choice.\n\n**Example-based explanations** retrieve similar training examples (\"the model treated you like these other cases\"). Intuitive, but easily misleading if the retrieved examples do not actually drive the decision.\n\nA key warning: **post-hoc methods can be wrong.** They approximate the model's behavior locally; they do not reveal the model's mechanism. An explanation that satisfies a stakeholder is not the same as an explanation that is accurate.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A SHAP report says feature X drove a denial. You re-run it after nudging one input by a rounding error and a different feature now ranks first. What does this instability most directly threaten?\" explanation=\"Instability under tiny input perturbations attacks faithfulness: if the attribution flips on a rounding error, it is not reliably reflecting the model's actual decision process. The lever might still be actionable and the format still context-appropriate, but you can no longer trust that it describes the model.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The faithfulness of the explanation\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The actionability of the explanation\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The contextual appropriateness of the explanation\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing; SHAP is exact by construction\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"explainability-stack\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## What Counts as a Meaningful Explanation\n\nWachter, Mittelstadt, and Russell (2018) argued that a meaningful explanation under GDPR should be **counterfactual and actionable**: it should tell the decision subject what would have produced a different decision, and that lever should be one the subject can act on. \"You were denied because of your zip code\" is legally fraught (the zip code is a proxy for protected attributes). \"You were denied because your debt-to-income ratio is 43%; below 38% would change the outcome\" is actionable.\n\nThree properties characterize a defensible explanation:\n\n- **Faithful**: the explanation accurately reflects the model's actual decision process, not a comforting paraphrase.\n- **Actionable**: the decision subject can do something with the explanation.\n- **Contextually appropriate**: the level of detail matches the stakeholder (regulator, decision subject, internal auditor) and the stakes.\n\nA faithful explanation that the subject cannot act on is a transparency floor, not a meaningful explanation. An actionable explanation that misrepresents the model is dangerous.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Case: Auditing a Credit-Denial Explanation\n\nReturn to the lending setting. A black-box model denies an applicant a loan. The compliance team must ship *one* explanation. Three candidates are on the table. We score each against the three properties and against the US Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA), which requires an \"adverse action notice\" stating specific, accurate reasons.\n\n**The three candidate explanations:**\n\n- **A.** \"Your application did not meet our internal credit criteria.\"\n- **B.** \"Top SHAP features for your denial: zip code, age, income.\"\n- **C.** \"You were denied because your debt-to-income ratio is 43%. Bringing it below 38%, with other factors held constant, would have changed the decision.\"\n\n**Stakeholder / tradeoff analysis:**\n\n| Property | A (boilerplate) | B (raw SHAP) | C (counterfactual) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Faithful | Unknown (says nothing checkable) | Possibly, but SHAP is local and unstable | Yes, if the threshold is validated against the model |\n| Actionable | No lever given | No clear action; cannot change age or zip | Clear, legitimate lever the subject controls |\n| Contextually appropriate | Fails the applicant; too vague for a regulator | Confuses the applicant; alarms the auditor | Matches applicant need and auditor scrutiny |\n| FCRA adverse-action fit | Non-compliant (no specific reason) | Risky: surfaces a protected proxy (zip) and age | Compliant: specific, actionable, neutral lever |\n| Discrimination exposure | Hides any proxy use | Advertises proxy use in writing | Routes around proxies to a legitimate factor |\n\n**Decision and reasoning.** Candidate **C** is the only one that clears all three properties and the FCRA bar simultaneously. Candidate **A** is the path of least engineering effort and is exactly the failure the Apple Card episode exposed: no decision-specific reason. Candidate **B** is tempting because it looks technical and rigorous, but it is the most legally dangerous of the three. It puts \"zip code\" and \"age\" in an official notice, where zip code is a well-known proxy for race and income, and age is itself a protected characteristic in credit. Surfacing them as the operative reasons can convert an opaque problem into a documented one.\n\n**The catch.** Choosing C is not the end of the duty. The team must verify that the counterfactual is *faithful*: that moving debt-to-income below 38% with everything else fixed would actually flip this model's output. If the model is nonlinear and entangled, a single clean threshold may not exist, and presenting one would be an actionable-but-unfaithful explanation, the dangerous quadrant. The honest path may be to validate the counterfactual per-case, or, following Rudin, to ask whether a glass-box model would have been competitive here in the first place.\n\n> **Key Insight:** The most defensible explanation is rarely the most technical-looking one. \"Faithful and actionable\" beats \"rigorous-sounding,\" and an explanation that names protected proxies in writing can be worse than no explanation at all.\n\nNow put yourself in the compliance seat. The denial has gone out, the applicant has filed a complaint, and you must choose what to ship and how to back it up. Each choice carries a downstream consequence the prior table only hinted at.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Shipping the Adverse-Action Notice\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"A black-box model denied an applicant. Engineering hands you three candidate notices. Which do you ship in the FCRA adverse-action letter?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A: \\\"Did not meet our internal credit criteria.\\\"\",\"next\":\"boiler\",\"feedback\":\"This is the Apple Card failure: no decision-specific reason.\"},{\"label\":\"B: \\\"Top SHAP features: zip code, age, income.\\\"\",\"next\":\"shap\",\"feedback\":\"You just put protected proxies in an official legal notice.\"},{\"label\":\"C: \\\"Debt-to-income is 43%; below 38% would change the outcome.\\\"\",\"next\":\"counter\",\"feedback\":\"Counterfactual and actionable, but you owe one more step.\"}]},\"boiler\":{\"prompt\":\"The regulator reviews the file. The notice states no specific, accurate reason.\",\"feedback\":\"Outcome: FCRA non-compliance. A vague notice is exactly the gap the Apple Card episode exposed. Go back and choose a notice that names a specific, checkable reason.\",\"choices\":[]},\"shap\":{\"prompt\":\"Plaintiff counsel reads the notice aloud in the complaint: the lender named zip code (a race proxy) and age (a protected credit characteristic) as the operative reasons.\",\"feedback\":\"Outcome: you converted an opaque problem into a documented one. Surfacing protected proxies in writing raises disparate-impact exposure rather than reducing it. The technical-looking choice was the most dangerous.\",\"choices\":[]},\"counter\":{\"prompt\":\"You chose the faithful-and-actionable lever. Before you sign, what must you verify?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Nothing; a counterfactual is self-justifying\",\"next\":\"unfaithful\",\"feedback\":\"Counterfactuals are not automatically faithful to a nonlinear model.\"},{\"label\":\"That moving DTI below 38% with all else fixed actually flips THIS model’s output\",\"next\":\"validated\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly: faithfulness must be tested per case.\"}]},\"unfaithful\":{\"prompt\":\"The model is entangled; no clean single threshold exists. The applicant lowers DTI to 37% and is still denied.\",\"feedback\":\"Outcome: actionable-but-unfaithful, the dangerous quadrant. You promised a lever that does not control the decision. Validate the counterfactual against the model, or follow Rudin and ask whether a glass-box model was competitive here.\",\"choices\":[]},\"validated\":{\"prompt\":\"You validate the threshold against the model per case and document it.\",\"feedback\":\"Outcome: faithful, actionable, contextually appropriate, and FCRA-compliant. This is the defensible path: the lever is real, the subject controls it, and no protected proxy appears in the notice.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nAfter working that decision tree, articulate the underlying principle in your own words.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Wachter et al. argue a meaningful explanation should be counterfactual and actionable. In your own words, why does 'we did not use the protected attribute' fail to satisfy a right to explanation even when it is literally true?\" answer=\"A right to explanation is about giving the decision subject a faithful, decision-specific reason they can act on, not about the inputs the model formally excluded. 'We did not use gender' is a statement about the feature list, not about why THIS decision came out as it did, and protected attributes can still be reconstructed through proxies like zip code. So the claim can be true and still leave the subject with no actionable, faithful reason, which is precisely the gap GDPR Recital 71 and the counterfactual framing try to close.\"></self-explain>\n\n---\n\n## Legal Scaffolding: GDPR, EU AI Act, FCRA\n\n**GDPR Article 22** restricts decisions based solely on automated processing where they produce legal or similarly significant effects. Recital 71 references meaningful information about the logic involved. The exact scope of this \"right to explanation\" is contested in the scholarship, but the regulatory direction is clear: opacity is not a defense.\n\n**EU AI Act** (2024) imposes transparency obligations on providers of high-risk AI systems and on providers of general-purpose AI. Technical documentation, instructions for use, and human oversight are required at the system-design layer, not bolted on after deployment.\n\n**Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA)** in the US requires adverse-action notices with specific reasons when credit is denied. AI-driven credit decisions inherit this obligation; vague reasons like candidate A above are not compliant.\n\nThese frameworks differ in scope but converge on a common commitment: **opaque high-stakes automation is not regulatorily defensible.** Engineers and instructional designers building such systems must produce explanation infrastructure as part of the build, not as an afterthought.\n\n---\n\n## Counterarguments and Limitations\n\nA rigorous treatment has to take the strongest objections seriously rather than wave them away.\n\n- **\"Interpretable models cost accuracy we cannot afford.\"** Sometimes true, especially in perception tasks like medical imaging where deep nets clearly outperform simple models. Rudin's reply is narrower than critics assume: she argues the gap is often *unmeasured and overstated* for structured, high-stakes tabular decisions (credit, recidivism, hiring), not that interpretability is free everywhere. The duty is to measure the gap, not to assume it.\n- **\"Explanations can be gamed.\"** If you tell people the exact lever, some will manipulate inputs (so-called gaming) rather than improve the underlying state. This is real, but it cuts against opacity-as-security, which is brittle, more than against transparency. Designers respond by surfacing levers tied to genuine creditworthiness rather than easily faked artifacts.\n- **\"A right to explanation may not legally exist.\"** Several scholars argue GDPR's binding text creates a right to be informed, not a robust right to a per-decision explanation. Granting the legal uncertainty, the EU AI Act and FCRA impose concrete documentation and notice duties regardless, so the engineering obligation does not vanish with the GDPR debate.\n- **\"Post-hoc explanations are good enough.\"** Only if validated. The honest limit is that a local approximation can be confidently wrong about the global mechanism, which is why faithfulness has to be tested, not presumed.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Right to Explanation\" context=\"A lender deploys a black-box credit-scoring model. A denied applicant requests an explanation. The vendor provides 'top features by SHAP value' which include zip code. Walk through whether this explanation is faithful, actionable, contextually appropriate, and legally defensible under FCRA.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\nBefore the bullet summary, map how this module's core ideas connect: which concepts are properties of the model versus the explanation surface, and which legal duties attach to which.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"interpretability\",\"explainability\",\"post_hoc_explanation\",\"counterfactual_explanation\",\"right_to_explanation\",\"gdpr_article_22\"]'></concept-map>\n\n- **Interpretability** is about the model; **explainability** is about the explanation layer wrapped around it. A black box can be explainable but never interpretable.\n- For high-stakes structured decisions, **prefer an inherently interpretable model when its accuracy is competitive** (Rudin 2019), and measure the gap rather than assume it.\n- Post-hoc methods (LIME, SHAP, counterfactuals, examples) **approximate** behavior locally and **can be unfaithful**; an explanation that reassures is not the same as one that is correct.\n- A defensible explanation is **faithful, actionable, and contextually appropriate**; the dangerous quadrant is actionable-but-unfaithful.\n- Putting **protected proxies** (zip code, age) into an official notice can be worse than a vague notice; counterfactual, lever-based reasons are usually the most defensible.\n- **GDPR Art. 22, the EU AI Act, and FCRA** converge: opaque high-stakes automation is not regulatorily defensible, and explanation infrastructure is part of the build.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nThe next module moves from *explaining one decision* to *accounting for a whole system*: audit trails, model documentation (model cards, datasheets), and the allocation of responsibility when an automated decision causes harm. Explainability is the per-decision floor; accountability is the institutional structure built on top of it.\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A counterfactual explanation says 'your loan was denied because your debt-to-income is 43%; below 38% would change the decision.' Why is this generally considered more defensible than 'top SHAP features were zip code, age, and income'?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"It is shorter.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"It is faithful, actionable (the subject can target a specific change), and avoids surfacing protected-attribute proxies (zip code) as the operative reason. SHAP features can be unfaithful, unactionable, and may surface protected proxies.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"SHAP is always wrong.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. Wachter et al. argue counterfactual, actionable explanations meet the spirit of the right to explanation; raw feature attributions often fail on actionability and can surface protected-attribute proxies inappropriately.\"></interactive-quiz>\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"explain_interp_vs_explain","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"interpretability","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["interpretability","explainability"],"stem":"Which statement most accurately distinguishes interpretability from explainability?","options":["They are synonyms.","Interpretability is a property of the model itself (auditable by structure); explainability is a property of an explanation surface layered around a possibly opaque model.","Interpretability applies only to deep networks.","Explainability is a legal term, interpretability is technical."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Interpretable models can be read directly. Explainable systems offer a generated rationale that may sit on top of an opaque model. The two properties can be present independently.","misconception_id":"interp_explain_same","learning_objective_ids":["ai-ethics-03-01-lo-01"]},{"id":"explain_counterfactual_value","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"counterfactual_explanation","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["counterfactual_explanation","right_to_explanation"],"stem":"Why are counterfactual explanations often considered more meaningful for decision subjects than raw feature attributions?","options":["Because they are always shorter.","Because they are actionable: they tell the subject what minimal change would have flipped the decision, giving them a lever they can act on. Raw feature attributions often fail on actionability and can surface protected-attribute proxies.","Because SHAP is always wrong.","Because counterfactual explanations are required by GDPR specifically."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Wachter, Mittelstadt, and Russell argue counterfactuals satisfy the spirit of meaningful explanation: faithful, actionable, and avoiding inappropriate surfacing of proxies.","misconception_id":"shap_is_always_meaningful","learning_objective_ids":["ai-ethics-03-01-lo-02","ai-ethics-03-01-lo-03","ai-ethics-03-01-lo-04"]},{"id":"explain_post_hoc_limit","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"post_hoc_explanation","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["post_hoc_explanation"],"stem":"What is the most important caution about post-hoc explanation methods like LIME or SHAP?","options":["They are always accurate by construction.","They approximate the model's behavior locally; an explanation that satisfies a stakeholder is not the same as an explanation that faithfully describes the model's mechanism. Post-hoc methods can be wrong.","They only work on linear models.","They are illegal under GDPR."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Post-hoc explanations approximate, not reveal. Faithful and satisfying are different properties; both matter and they can come apart.","misconception_id":"post_hoc_is_truth","learning_objective_ids":["ai-ethics-03-01-lo-03"]},{"id":"explain_high_stakes_choice","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"interpretability","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["interpretability"],"stem":"Cynthia Rudin's central argument about high-stakes decisions is best summarized as:","options":["Always use the largest model possible.","Prefer inherently interpretable models for high-stakes decisions when their accuracy is competitive; many published comparisons overstate the accuracy gap and ignore the cost of unauditable explanations.","Black-box models are always more accurate.","Interpretability does not matter outside research."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Rudin (Nature Machine Intelligence, 2019) argues for interpretable-first design in high-stakes settings, against the assumption that accuracy requires opacity.","misconception_id":"blackbox_always_more_accurate","learning_objective_ids":["ai-ethics-03-01-lo-01"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"interp_explain_same","pattern":"term_collapse","description":"Treating interpretability and explainability as synonyms","trigger":"learner uses the two terms interchangeably without distinction","feedback":"Interpretability is a property of the model (auditable by structure). Explainability is a property of an explanation surface around a possibly opaque model. The two can be present independently.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"shap_is_always_meaningful","pattern":"tool_legitimization","description":"Treating SHAP or LIME outputs as automatically constituting a meaningful explanation","trigger":"learner provides raw feature attributions to a denied applicant as the legal explanation","feedback":"Meaningful explanations are faithful, actionable, and contextually appropriate. Raw feature attributions can fail on actionability and may inappropriately surface protected-attribute proxies.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"post_hoc_is_truth","pattern":"approximation_collapse","description":"Believing post-hoc explanation methods reveal the model's actual mechanism","trigger":"learner cites SHAP output as proof of why the model made a decision","feedback":"Post-hoc methods approximate model behavior locally. They can be wrong. A satisfying explanation is not necessarily a faithful explanation.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"blackbox_always_more_accurate","pattern":"accuracy_assumption","description":"Assuming black-box models are always more accurate than interpretable alternatives","trigger":"learner argues 'we need a black box for accuracy' without testing interpretable baselines","feedback":"Rudin (2019) argues that for high-stakes decisions, inherently interpretable models are often competitive in accuracy and dominate on auditability. Test before assuming.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"53a1a69206b1df0dd7ded6559c8a1f9e70f10eab8c62104baa7043ee970e92af","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}